{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weighted incidental comorbidities (e.g., seizure history or diabetes) and/or chose by superficial match rather than verifying that the option is actually indicated for the stated condition. It also missed situations where the correct choice is the option that is itself a first-line disease-specific therapy, even when a comorbidity warrants caution but not avoidance.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_primacy",
      "text": "First anchor on the stated condition and ask which option is a recognized treatment for that condition. Only then apply patient-specific safety filters. Never let an incidental comorbidity or current medication override the need for a clearly indicated option."
    },
    {
      "id": "comorbidity_caution_not_ban",
      "text": "A comorbidity or concurrent drug that requires caution does not automatically make a drug unacceptable. Distinguish 'contraindicated/avoid' from 'use with caution/monitor.' Only exclude an otherwise indicated drug when the patient-specific risk is a true contraindication relative to the alternatives."
    },
    {
      "id": "route_and_systemic_risk",
      "text": "When hormone-related or similar therapies are considered, distinguish local/topical from systemic formulations. Prefer the route with lower systemic exposure when the patient has a history such as hormone-sensitive cancer or seizure disorder, provided the local route is still indicated."
    },
    {
      "id": "active_problem_over_history",
      "text": "Treat the current presenting problem as the primary indication. Historical conditions matter only insofar as they change safety or choice among indicated treatments, not as a reason to avoid all treatment."
    },
    {
      "id": "disease_specific_first_line",
      "text": "For each condition, identify its recognized first-line or standard therapy class. Do not substitute an unrelated drug simply because it has some loose symptomatic overlap or because the first-line drug has a manageable interaction."
    },
    {
      "id": "symptom_mechanism_map",
      "text": "Translate symptom clusters into the underlying mechanism before choosing: e.g., bronchospasm implies bronchodilator need, delayed gastric emptying implies prokinetic need, ocular inflammation implies anti-inflammatory need. Match drug mechanism to mechanism, not to the patient's unrelated comorbidities."
    },
    {
      "id": "incidental_medication_irrelevance",
      "text": "A current medication is relevant only if it interacts with the candidate drug or affects the disease being treated. Otherwise, do not let it steer selection away from the indicated option."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "indicated for the stated condition",
    "first-line therapy",
    "contraindicated versus cautions",
    "local versus systemic exposure",
    "caution does not equal avoid",
    "incidental comorbidity",
    "mechanism match"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated active condition, current medicines, and patient facts. Classify each fact as relevant to indication, relevant to safety, or incidental. Do not elevate incidental history into a decisive factor.",
    "safety_check": "Separate true contraindications from cautions requiring monitoring. Consider route and systemic exposure when a history raises risk. Do not let a manageable interaction remove a clearly indicated option.",
    "option_selection": "Prefer the option that matches the active condition's standard therapy. Among indicated options, choose the safest formulation/route for the patient's history. Exclude only when contraindicated or when a safer indicated alternative exists."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weighted the first plausible indication it recognized and under-used the full patient picture: it missed that the best option must match the specific disease subtype, prior treatment response, active infection/immunosuppression, and drug-class role, while also failing to reject options that are unrelated or contraindicated. It also sometimes treated a mentioned medicine as the answer rather than as a safety/context clue.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_specificity",
      "text": "Match the option to the most specific stated diagnosis or subtype, not just the broad disease category. If the case names a molecular subtype, stage, or line of therapy, prefer the option indicated for that exact context."
    },
    {
      "id": "prior_treatment_response",
      "text": "When the patient is already taking a medicine for the target condition and reports inadequate response, do not choose that same medicine again unless the question is explicitly about optimization. Prefer a different guideline-supported alternative in the same therapeutic class or a clearly indicated next step."
    },
    {
      "id": "infection_immunosuppression_safety",
      "text": "In immunosuppressed or transplant patients with suspected infection, avoid immunosuppressants and steroids that could worsen infection; prioritize the anti-infective indicated for the suspected organism. Conversely, in suspected transplant rejection without active infection, prioritize the immunosuppressant used for rejection rather than an unrelated drug."
    },
    {
      "id": "symptom_vs_disease_modifier",
      "text": "Distinguish symptom-relief options from disease-modifying or definitive therapy. For chronic conditions, choose the option that treats the underlying disease process when the question asks for medication choice, not merely a urinary analgesic or symptomatic agent."
    },
    {
      "id": "unrelated_drug_rejection",
      "text": "Reject options from unrelated therapeutic classes even if they are familiar or commonly used. An antibiotic, antidepressant, anesthetic, or cancer drug is not correct unless its mechanism matches the stated condition."
    },
    {
      "id": "mentioned_medicine_as_context",
      "text": "Treat a medicine already named by the patient as context for safety, interaction, or prior failure, not automatically as the answer. The correct option may be a different drug that addresses the same problem more appropriately."
    },
    {
      "id": "contraindication_first",
      "text": "Before selecting an option, check for patient-specific contraindications such as active infection, immunosuppression, organ dysfunction, or interacting current medicines. Eliminate options that are unsafe in that context even if they are otherwise indicated."
    },
    {
      "id": "class_role_matching",
      "text": "Identify the therapeutic role required by the case: antiviral, immunosuppressant, PDE5 inhibitor, targeted cancer therapy, etc. Choose the option whose class and role match the diagnosis, and do not substitute a drug from a different role."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "specific diagnosis subtype",
    "prior treatment failure",
    "active infection",
    "immunosuppression",
    "contraindication",
    "disease-modifying therapy",
    "unrelated drug class",
    "current medication context"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the stated diagnosis or suspected diagnosis, including subtype, stage, mutation, or line of therapy. Record current medicines, prior treatment response, active infections, transplant status, organ function, and relevant history. Do not infer missing facts.",
    "safety_check": "Screen each option against patient-specific contraindications and interactions: active infection, immunosuppression, organ dysfunction, current medicines, and prior failure. Eliminate unsafe or ineffective options before comparing efficacy.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option that matches the most specific indication and the required therapeutic role, while respecting prior treatment response and safety. Prefer disease-modifying therapy over symptomatic relief when the question asks for treatment of the underlying condition, and reject unrelated drug classes."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent often picked the option matching the inferred diagnosis while ignoring the patient's active medication burden and liver/immune constraints, and it sometimes treated the most familiar drug in a class as automatically safest without comparing metabolism or indication fit.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "First name the diagnosis or syndrome from the stated symptoms, then require the selected drug to have a recognized indication for that exact problem; eliminate options that belong to unrelated therapeutic classes."
    },
    {
      "id": "medication_burden",
      "text": "Always list every current medicine the patient reports before choosing. If a candidate would add overlapping toxicity, immune suppression, or central nervous system depression to an existing drug, prefer a non-interacting or lower-risk alternative."
    },
    {
      "id": "hepatic_caution",
      "text": "When the patient has liver disease, hepatitis history, or past elevated liver enzymes, avoid or minimize drugs with significant hepatic metabolism or hepatotoxic potential unless no safer indicated option exists; favor agents with less hepatic burden."
    },
    {
      "id": "infection_immunosuppression",
      "text": "If an infection is suspected in a patient on corticosteroids or other immunosuppressants, do not choose another immunosuppressant; select an antimicrobial appropriate for the suspected organism and check for interactions with the current regimen."
    },
    {
      "id": "refractory_vs_prophylaxis",
      "text": "For seizure or chronic episodic disorders, distinguish failure of current therapy from new-onset disease. If the current drug is not controlling the condition, choose an indicated add-on or alternative rather than unrelated symptomatic or hormonal agents."
    },
    {
      "id": "class_safety_compare",
      "text": "When two options are in the same therapeutic class, compare their specific indications, metabolism, and repeat-use safety rather than choosing the only familiar name. The safer class member depends on the patient's organ function and the labeled indication."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_fact_not_present",
      "text": "Do not assume a contraindication, test result, pregnancy, renal failure, or allergy that was not stated. Use only the dialogue facts and general drug knowledge, and state uncertainty when a decisive fact is absent."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_answer_shortcuts",
      "text": "Do not use benchmark position, case identifiers, or memorized answer letters. The choice must follow from indication, patient-specific safety, and current medication burden."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "current medications",
    "liver disease",
    "immunosuppression",
    "infection",
    "refractory symptoms",
    "hepatic metabolism",
    "contraindication",
    "indication"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Record the stated diagnosis or syndrome, all current medicines, and any organ-function or immune-related history. Distinguish facts actually given from absent facts, and do not infer unstated contraindications.",
    "safety_check": "Check each option against the patient's current medicines and comorbidities, especially liver disease, immunosuppression, and infection. Avoid adding overlapping toxicity or an agent that worsens the existing condition.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option with the clearest indication for the stated problem and the best patient-specific safety profile. For same-class options, compare metabolism, labeled use, and repeat-treatment risk rather than familiarity. Exclude options from unrelated classes."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-anchored on the first plausible diagnosis and on the patient's existing medication, then selected a drug by superficial familiarity rather than by matching the specific indication, route/formulation, and patient-specific contraindications. It also failed to distinguish drugs that treat a condition from drugs that worsen it, and it sometimes treated a missing fact as if it were present.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "Before comparing options, restate the working diagnosis and the specific treatment goal (e.g., anti-inflammatory, antiviral, antipsychotic, glucose rescue). Eliminate any option that does not treat that goal, even if it is familiar or commonly used elsewhere."
    },
    {
      "id": "route_and_formulation",
      "text": "When the diagnosis implies a local or procedure-specific treatment, check whether the option is appropriate by route and formulation for that use. A drug that is a reasonable systemic corticosteroid may still be wrong if the condition requires intralesional injection or a different formulation."
    },
    {
      "id": "contraindication_direction",
      "text": "For each candidate, ask whether it could worsen the suspected condition or interact with the patient's current medicines. Do not select a drug that lowers glucose in hypoglycemia, suppresses immunity in an active infection, or adds GI/bleeding risk when the patient already has GI symptoms or is on aspirin."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_medication_check",
      "text": "List the patient's current medicines and check each candidate against them. If a candidate has a known interaction with a current medicine, prefer a safer alternative unless the interaction is clearly manageable and the alternative is not indicated."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_fact_rule",
      "text": "If a patient fact needed for a safety decision is not stated, do not assume it is present or absent. Either avoid options whose safety depends on that missing fact or state the uncertainty and choose the option that is safest under the known facts."
    },
    {
      "id": "duplicate_ingredient_avoidance",
      "text": "Do not choose an option that is the same drug or same ingredient as a medicine the patient is already taking when the question asks for an additional or alternative treatment, unless the scenario explicitly calls for continuing or optimizing that same drug."
    },
    {
      "id": "diagnosis_specific_antipsychotic",
      "text": "For acute psychotic symptoms, select an antipsychotic that is indicated for the presentation and compatible with current medicines. Do not substitute an unrelated biologic or an antiemetic for the antipsychotic effect."
    },
    {
      "id": "diagnosis_specific_antiviral",
      "text": "When a viral infection such as herpes-zoster is suspected in an immunosuppressed patient, prioritize an antiviral active against that virus. Avoid steroids or immunosuppressants that could worsen the infection unless a specific indication and safety plan are present."
    },
    {
      "id": "diagnosis_specific_endocrine",
      "text": "For suspected adrenal insufficiency with orthostatic symptoms and weight loss, choose mineralocorticoid or glucocorticoid replacement as appropriate. Do not choose anxiolytics or antidepressants as substitutes for hormone replacement."
    },
    {
      "id": "diagnosis_specific_rheumatologic",
      "text": "For inflammatory arthritis with skin and GI involvement, prefer a targeted immunomodulator indicated for the suspected disease. Avoid NSAIDs when GI symptoms are prominent, and avoid corticosteroids as a default long-term choice when a disease-specific agent is available."
    },
    {
      "id": "hypoglycemia_rescue",
      "text": "In hypoglycemia, the treatment goal is rapid glucose elevation. Choose a glucagon rescue product or rapid glucose source; never choose a sulfonylurea or other glucose-lowering drug."
    },
    {
      "id": "option_elimination_order",
      "text": "Use a consistent elimination order: first remove options that do not treat the working diagnosis, then remove options contraindicated by patient-specific factors or current medicines, then compare the remaining options by route, formulation, and guideline preference."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "working diagnosis",
    "treatment goal",
    "route and formulation",
    "contraindication direction",
    "current medication interaction",
    "missing patient fact",
    "duplicate ingredient",
    "glucose rescue",
    "antiviral",
    "antipsychotic",
    "mineralocorticoid replacement",
    "targeted immunomodulator"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated condition, current medicines, and relevant patient facts. Separate confirmed facts from missing facts; do not fill gaps with assumptions. Note the specific treatment goal implied by the diagnosis.",
    "safety_check": "Check each candidate against patient-specific contraindications, current medicines, and the direction of effect. Reject options that could worsen the suspected condition or interact with current therapy. If a needed fact is missing, treat it as unknown and avoid options whose safety depends on it.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option that best satisfies both the indication and patient-specific safety. Eliminate non-indicated options first, then contraindicated options, then compare remaining options by route, formulation, and guideline preference. Do not choose a duplicate of a current medicine unless continuation is explicitly intended."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent fails when a salient comorbidity, recent procedure, or current medication is treated as decisive without first confirming the primary problem and identifying the safest first-line treatment. It overweights theoretical interactions and ignores that options must match the actual indication, patient-specific contraindications, and the standard preferred therapy.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "mechanism_over_label",
      "text": "When choosing a medication, prioritize the mechanism of action needed by the stated diagnosis and the patient's active problem; do not let a secondary comorbidity or theoretical interaction override the absence of indication."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_facts_are_missing",
      "text": "If a patient fact is not stated (allergy, renal function, pregnancy, culture result), do not assume it exists or use it to eliminate a standard therapy; choose based on verified evidence and note the uncertainty."
    },
    {
      "id": "primary_indication_first",
      "text": "Rank options by whether they treat the primary diagnosed condition; agents that do not address that condition should be deprioritized even if they seem safer for another comorbidity."
    },
    {
      "id": "safety_without_deletion",
      "text": "Use patient-specific contraindications to reject only medications that are truly unsafe, not merely less ideal; when multiple options are unsafe, pick the one that best balances efficacy for the primary indication with the stated safety constraints."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_med_context",
      "text": "Interpret the current medication list as context for interaction risk and comorbidity, but do not let it distract from the main treatment target; only avoid a medication when the interaction is clinically significant for the stated patient."
    },
    {
      "id": "procedure_risk_nuance",
      "text": "After recent surgery or neuraxial procedures, anticoagulants may carry risk, but if the primary problem is thrombosis and no non-anticoagulant treats it, choose the safest anticoagulant option rather than substituting an unrelated drug."
    },
    {
      "id": "hyperthyroid_rate_control",
      "text": "In supraventricular tachycardia with hyperthyroidism, prefer a beta-blocker for rate control and hyperthyroid symptom relief; calcium-channel blockers and antidepressants are not equivalent choices for this primary problem."
    },
    {
      "id": "intra_abdominal_coverage",
      "text": "For peritonitis, select an antibiotic with broad gram-negative and anaerobic intra-abdominal coverage; a drug that lacks that spectrum is not made appropriate by the current medication history alone."
    },
    {
      "id": "lipid_target_matching",
      "text": "For mixed hyperlipidemia with elevated LDL and VLDL, a statin is the preferred first-line agent; triglyceride-focused fibrates and non-indicated drugs should not displace statin therapy unless a specific contraindication is stated."
    },
    {
      "id": "immunosuppression_gvhd",
      "text": "In graft-versus-host disease not controlled on a biologic, use an established immunosuppressant for GVHD; do not choose a drug for an unrelated indication just because the biologic is already present."
    },
    {
      "id": "analgesic_selection",
      "text": "For headaches and musculoskeletal pain, start with the safest effective analgesic for the patient; when stimulant medication is present, avoid unnecessary NSAID risk and prefer acetaminophen unless a specific reason requires an anti-inflammatory."
    },
    {
      "id": "infection_with_cardiac_history",
      "text": "For early syphilis with possible cardiac concerns, use the recommended therapy for the infection while respecting cardiac safety; do not withhold standard antibiotic treatment based on unverified cardiac facts."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "primary indication",
    "first-line therapy",
    "contraindication",
    "interaction",
    "hyperthyroidism",
    "peritonitis",
    "hyperlipidemia",
    "graft-versus-host disease",
    "DVT prophylaxis after surgery",
    "syphilis"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the primary diagnosis or problem, current medicines, allergies, organ function, recent procedures, and other explicitly stated risks. Distinguish stated facts from absent facts; do not infer a contraindication or missing result.",
    "safety_check": "Check only stated patient-specific contraindications and clinically significant interactions. A theoretical or unspecified risk should not eliminate a standard therapy; if the primary condition requires a drug class, choose the safest option within that class.",
    "option_selection": "Select the option that best treats the primary indication while respecting verified patient safety. Prefer first-line therapy for the diagnosis; reject options that do not address the primary problem. If the best option has a manageable risk, pair it with appropriate monitoring rather than substituting an unrelated drug."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weighted prior medication exposure as a contraindication, under-used the stated diagnosis to drive indication, and sometimes chose a plausible but non-preferred or non-indicated option. It also failed to distinguish symptom relief from disease-specific treatment and to prioritize guideline-preferred therapy when multiple options were plausible.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "diagnosis_driven_indication",
      "text": "First anchor on the stated or suspected diagnosis, then select the option whose primary indication matches that diagnosis. Do not let a prior or current medication automatically exclude an otherwise indicated therapy unless a true contraindication or interaction is stated."
    },
    {
      "id": "retreatment_not_contraindication",
      "text": "Prior exposure to a biologic or disease-specific therapy is not by itself a contraindication to retreatment. If the diagnosis still calls for that class and no safety issue is stated, consider it a valid option."
    },
    {
      "id": "symptom_vs_disease_treatment",
      "text": "Distinguish treating the underlying disease from treating a symptom or side effect. When the diagnosis is clear, prefer the disease-specific therapy over a symptomatic or supportive measure unless the question is explicitly about symptom control."
    },
    {
      "id": "preferred_guideline_option",
      "text": "When several options are plausible, prefer the guideline-preferred or first-line agent for the stated condition. Do not choose a less preferred alternative merely because it is also active or safer in a general sense."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_check_without_invention",
      "text": "Check interactions only against facts actually stated. Do not infer a contraindication from a medication class or from a missing fact. If no interaction is documented in the case, do not create one."
    },
    {
      "id": "comorbidity_safety_balance",
      "text": "When a comorbidity or current medicine is stated, use it to rank options by safety, but do not let it override a strong indication unless the risk is explicit. Choose the option that best satisfies both indication and stated safety constraints."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_unrelated_or_oncology_options",
      "text": "Eliminate options whose primary use is unrelated to the stated diagnosis, such as antineoplastics for infection or hormone therapy for infection. Match the drug class to the problem."
    },
    {
      "id": "infection_antibiotic_selection",
      "text": "For suspected infection, choose an antibiotic with appropriate coverage for the likely organism and site. In cystic fibrosis pneumonia, consider Pseudomonas coverage when the history suggests it, rather than a narrower or non-antibiotic option."
    },
    {
      "id": "complement_disorder_specifics",
      "text": "For complement-mediated disorders, prefer complement-directed therapy over unrelated immunomodulators. Do not substitute a TNF inhibitor for a complement inhibitor when the diagnosis is a complement disorder."
    },
    {
      "id": "diuretic_and_fluid_retention_context",
      "text": "For fluid retention with hypertension, consider diuretic choice in the context of current heart failure therapy. Avoid NSAIDs that can worsen fluid retention and blood pressure when a safer diuretic option is available."
    },
    {
      "id": "constipation_symptom_match",
      "text": "For constipation with bloating, prefer a laxative indicated for constipation. Do not choose antibiotics for hepatic encephalopathy or antineoplastics unless the case specifically supports those indications."
    },
    {
      "id": "lipid_lowering_priority",
      "text": "For hypercholesterolemia with chest tightness, prefer a statin as first-line lipid-lowering therapy unless contraindicated. Do not choose antihypertensives or PCSK9 inhibitors as the primary option when a statin is appropriate."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "indication",
    "contraindication",
    "interaction",
    "first-line",
    "disease-specific",
    "symptomatic",
    "retreatment",
    "complement-mediated",
    "Pseudomonas",
    "statin"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated or suspected diagnosis, current medicines, prior relevant exposures, and any explicitly stated comorbidities or symptoms. Do not treat a missing fact as present.",
    "safety_check": "Check only stated contraindications and interactions. Prior exposure or a drug class alone is not a contraindication unless the case says so. Use comorbidities to rank safety, not to invent exclusions.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option whose primary indication matches the diagnosis, preferring guideline-preferred or disease-specific therapy. Eliminate unrelated drug classes. When multiple options are plausible, prefer the one that best satisfies both indication and stated safety."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weighted the first plausible indication and under-used patient-specific safety modifiers (age, organ function, pregnancy, interactions, contraindications) and drug-specific dosing/route constraints. It also treated inferred or absent facts as present and failed to compare options on safety first, then efficacy.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "Start by naming the most likely active problem from the stated symptoms and history. Then require the chosen drug to have a recognized indication for that problem; if no option is indicated, do not force a choice."
    },
    {
      "id": "patient_safety_filter",
      "text": "Before selecting, filter every option through the patient's stated age, pregnancy/lactation status, renal/hepatic function, allergies, current medicines, and comorbidities. Exclude any option with a stated contraindication or dangerous interaction; do not assume a missing fact is normal."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_check",
      "text": "When the patient is already taking a medicine, check for clinically important interactions with each candidate. Prefer an option without a stated or well-known dangerous interaction; if all options interact, choose the one with the least severe or most manageable interaction."
    },
    {
      "id": "organ_function",
      "text": "If renal or hepatic impairment is stated or strongly implied, adjust or avoid options that require dose reduction or are contraindicated. Do not choose a hepatically cleared or renally cleared drug without confirming suitability."
    },
    {
      "id": "age_and_pregnancy",
      "text": "For pediatric, elderly, pregnant, or breastfeeding patients, exclude options that are contraindicated or preferred against in that group. If the patient's age or pregnancy status is not stated, do not invent it, but also do not ignore it when the dialogue clearly implies a risk group."
    },
    {
      "id": "route_and_formulation",
      "text": "Match the option to the required route and setting. If the scenario calls for oral, topical, inhaled, or intravenous therapy, eliminate options that cannot be given by that route or are inappropriate for the care setting."
    },
    {
      "id": "dose_and_monitoring",
      "text": "Prefer options whose dosing and monitoring burden fit the patient's situation. Avoid drugs that require intensive monitoring or have a narrow therapeutic index when a safer alternative is available and indicated."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_duplicate_class",
      "text": "If the patient is already on a drug from a class, do not add another drug from the same class unless the scenario specifically requires combination or replacement. Check for therapeutic duplication."
    },
    {
      "id": "compare_not_anchor",
      "text": "Do not stop at the first option that seems plausible. Compare all options on indication, safety, interactions, and patient-specific factors, then choose the one that best satisfies the full set of constraints."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_fact_rule",
      "text": "If a fact needed for a safe choice is missing, treat it as unknown rather than present or absent. Choose the option that is safest under the stated facts and note what would change the decision if the missing fact were known."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_benchmark_shortcuts",
      "text": "Do not use case identifiers, answer letters, or benchmark-position patterns. Base every choice only on the clinical content in the dialogue and general drug knowledge."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "indication",
    "contraindication",
    "drug interaction",
    "renal impairment",
    "hepatic impairment",
    "pregnancy",
    "age",
    "route",
    "monitoring",
    "therapeutic duplication",
    "missing fact"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the stated condition, symptoms, current medicines, allergies, age, pregnancy/lactation status, renal/hepatic function, and relevant comorbidities. Mark any needed fact that is not stated as unknown rather than assuming it is normal.",
    "safety_check": "For each candidate, check stated contraindications, drug interactions, organ-function restrictions, age/pregnancy warnings, and monitoring requirements. Exclude options with a stated or well-known dangerous mismatch; do not invent facts to justify or reject an option.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option that is indicated for the active problem and has the best patient-specific safety profile after filtering for interactions, organ function, age/pregnancy, route, and monitoring. Compare all options rather than anchoring on the first plausible one."
  }
}

 {
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-trusted the clinician's stated diagnosis or the most salient drug name and ignored whether the chosen option actually treats the patient's active problem, whether a safer same-class alternative exists, and whether prior/current therapy changes the best next choice.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "Before choosing, restate the active problem in plain terms and require the selected drug to have a direct indication for that problem; reject options that are only related to a symptom, a past condition, or a drug class mentioned in the dialogue."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_salience_trap",
      "text": "Do not select an option merely because its name or class appears in the clinician's reasoning; verify that the option is the treatment for the patient's current diagnosis, not just a drug the clinician mentioned as background."
    },
    {
      "id": "safer_alternative_within_class",
      "text": "When two options share an indication, prefer the one with fewer patient-specific risks, such as less blood-pressure elevation, less need for monitoring, or fewer interactions with current medicines."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_medication_context",
      "text": "Treat current medicines as constraints: if the patient is already on a drug for the same problem, do not add a duplicate or interacting agent unless the new option offers a clear advantage and is safe with the existing regimen."
    },
    {
      "id": "prior_therapy_does_not_contraindicate",
      "text": "A history of prior exposure to a biologic or drug does not by itself rule out retreatment; assess whether the current problem is a relapse of the same condition and whether retreatment is indicated."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_fact_not_present",
      "text": "If a key safety fact is not stated, do not assume it is absent or normal; either avoid drugs that depend on that fact or note the uncertainty rather than inventing reassurance."
    },
